<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Using the button</h2>
  <h3>One button, three things.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    skyBlip Go has one button worth learning, and it says three things: a press
    changes page, two quick presses authorise what the device is asking about, and
    a hold of two seconds switches it off. Nothing else is hidden behind it, and
    the three never overlap &mdash; a hold is never read as a press, and a press
    made before a question appeared can never answer it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The case carries two more inputs: a small <span class="mono">RST</span>
    button, which is the processor&rsquo;s reset and the way into the USB
    bootloader, and a capacitive touch pad, which the firmware does not read at
    all. Both are covered at the foot of this page.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Gesture</th>
          <th>What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>One press</th>
          <td>next page &mdash; or, at a question on the glass, refuses it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Two presses, under 0.6&nbsp;s apart</th>
          <td>allows the question on the glass; on the settings page, changes the focused row</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Hold, 2&nbsp;s</th>
          <td>switches the device off</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

